Reading: the empirical n_cross(y) values (predecessor's own,
threshold ratio<0.5) fall COMFORTABLY below the rigorous upper bound
derived here from first principles via the CORRECTED, constant kernel
bound M=sqrt(pi/2)+eps -- as expected for a worst-case, crude
sup-norm bound (which discards all sign/cancellation structure the
true iterated kernel has). Both are LINEAR in y (matching
qualitatively), but the rigorous slope (e*M ~ 3.4-3.5) is roughly
5-7x the empirically measured slope (~0.5-0.77) -- an honest,
quantified gap, consistent with (and now quantified far more
precisely than) the predecessor's own honest observation that the
crude bound over-predicts the true growth rate.
